WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.642 --> 00:00:08.350
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to another episode

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:11.710
of "VM End-to-End," a show where
we have a former, maybe even

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.640
reformed VM skeptic and a
VM enthusiast, my friend

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.610
Brian, together to hash
out all things VMs.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.115
Brian, thank you for being here.

00:00:21.115 --> 00:00:22.240
What is this episode about?

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.050
You just told me it's
special, I had to be here,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:24.717
and that was it.

00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:27.130
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, I think
you're going to like this.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:33.643
It's where I admit that VMs
might not always be the answer.

00:00:33.643 --> 00:00:35.560
CARTER MORGAN: Can you
say that one more time?

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.890
BRIAN DORSEY: VMs might
not always be the answer.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:39.580
It depends.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.830
And so with that
in mind, I wanted

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.320
to bring in an expert
colleague of ours

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.400
who has been a
sysadmin, who has run

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.967
all kinds of different
machines, and who's

00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:53.050
now focused on serverless.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.560
Welcome, Katie.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.560
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Hello.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.398
Thank you for
inviting me, Brian.

00:00:58.398 --> 00:00:59.190
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.480
And Katie, I'm extra excited.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.980
I don't know if
you-- yeah, you just

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.610
heard what Brian said
about maybe VMs not always

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.500
being the answer.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.280
Didn't you get to play around
with the Migrate for Cloud Run

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:11.790
tooling?

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.230
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: I did.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.850
Brian, have you
heard about this one?

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.168
It's very cool.

00:01:17.168 --> 00:01:19.210
BRIAN DORSEY: Honestly,
I think I heard about it.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.730
And let me make sure I'm right.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.000
Is this the thing
that claims to be

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.700
able to make
containerized setups out

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.590
of what's running on a VM?

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.690
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
Yes, and it works.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.220
I've tried it.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.690
BRIAN DORSEY: Wait, OK.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.050
OK, I have to admit, I
was so skeptical of this.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.140
It's so much often
considered an anti-pattern.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.903
And you can't just lift this
whole VM into a container.

00:01:42.903 --> 00:01:44.820
Containers aren't VMs,
all this sort of stuff,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.820
that I didn't actually look at
it as carefully as I probably

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.050
should, because it didn't
pass my is-this-real test.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.030
So how was I wrong about that?

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.300
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: I had
the same sort of thought.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.240
It's like, but if I've
got a complex VM that's

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.970
got all these different
services-- some of them

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.950
are socket-driven,
some of them are

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.430
port-driven-- how am I
going to take that from a VM

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.289
and put it into a container?

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.960
But I found that it was
actually really cleverly done.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:22.650
What I did was, I built a very
special bespoke crystalline VM,

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.640
and I installed Postgres
from system packages.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.690
I set that all up.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.900
I set up a Django
website on there.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.450
I had a whole bunch of
user uploads on there.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.840
So it was a very bespoke VM.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.400
And then I ran it through
Migrate for Cloud Run.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.220
And that was able to
containerize the VM,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.530
identify what
services were running.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:50.280
And then I had the choice
to disable all the services

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.160
and then re-create them.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.760
Well, not recreate,
redefine the services.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.330
There's a particular YAML
that you can define--

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:05.400
services-confic.yaml-- where,
like you would define a whole

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.840
bunch of systemd services,
you can define new services.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.260
So I disable all
the services that's

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.670
running on this
containerized VM.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.860
And then I declare a
new one, which is just

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.290
my web service on a port.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.540
And then when that's
running in Cloud Run,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.220
all I do is say, see that port?

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.690
Go listen to that.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.940
One thing I did find, though--
that this worked really

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.790
well when combining it with
the database migration service.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:34.320
So I could take my
locally-running Postgres

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.410
database and then migrate
that into Cloud SQL.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:40.560
So I could migrate my VM twice.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.040
But it meant that I was
able to go from one very

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.740
special VM that if it went
away, I'd be very sad,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.180
into managed Cloud SQL and
then into a container in Cloud

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.270
Run that can scale to
zero, survive restarts, all

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:55.350
that kind of fun.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:56.890
It was actually really neat.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.280
I'm interested in
sharing more about that

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.930
to some of our customers.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:01.560
CARTER MORGAN:
There's two things

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.602
I want to ask about here.

00:04:02.602 --> 00:04:04.230
One, first, you
defined the mapping

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.020
between ports and
sockets and whatnot.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.570
But two, you said
that you had a VM,

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.090
or you wrote a
custom bespoke VM,

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.419
and then you transitioned it
into a more managed service.

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:16.919
Why?

00:04:16.919 --> 00:04:19.649
Why would you go up
the stack that way?

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:22.200
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Well,
Carter, what do you

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.400
want to have to worry about?

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.650
CARTER MORGAN: Me, personally?

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:26.250
Nothing.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.740
I want to write code, and
I want to be done with it.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.944
That's what I want
to worry about.

00:04:30.944 --> 00:04:34.590
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: For
brand-new greenfield projects

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.570
or proof-of-concept
projects, you

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.940
can do that straight away
with Cloud Run, where

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.130
it's such an abstraction
on top of the VM

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.480
on top of the Kubernetes
on top of everything.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.760
You just need to containerize
your code and go run it.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.928
But there are going to
be brownfield projects

00:04:49.928 --> 00:04:51.970
where you're going to have
something that exists.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.290
And if you want to pull it
up into a serverless system,

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:58.650
you're going to have to
do that migration step.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.460
But with managed
services, you don't

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.000
have to worry about
a number of things.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.500
Like, who were you talking
with the other day?

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.760
Was it Gabe?

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.027
About databases?

00:05:15.027 --> 00:05:16.110
BRIAN DORSEY: Yes, it was.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.150
He talked to us about
databases, hosted,

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.890
how to decide when
to host on a VM,

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.410
and some database
migration stuff.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.740
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Yeah, in
my bespoke virtual machine,

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:31.320
I would have to worry about
maintenance and system package

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.700
updates, and backups
of my database.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.380
Once I'm in Cloud SQL,
that's all there by default.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.300
What do you want to
have to worry about?

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.050
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.950
Well, let's get an example.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.220
Let's ground it in reality.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.980
Say one of our two
co-hosts on this show

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.680
was writing a small
back-end application.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:55.250
No front end.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:59.570
It just uses APIs, goes from
a command-line type deal.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:01.310
Where would I want to run that?

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.070
Or, sorry, where would our
co-host, whoever it is,

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.900
want to run that?

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:11.540
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Well, Carter
and/or Brian, it depends.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:15.385
It sounds like you may not even
have to worry about Cloud Run,

00:06:15.385 --> 00:06:16.760
because with Cloud
Run, you would

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.330
have to have a container
that listens on a port

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:21.980
and then serves stuff.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.250
But it sounds like if
you've just got a function,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.780
you might just
want to go straight

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:28.790
to Cloud Functions,
where you just

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:35.750
supply a function,
a method, a function

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:37.250
description with parameters.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:38.810
And then it gives you--

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:40.400
it returns data.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.760
And just copy, paste that
up into Cloud Functions,

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.110
and everything else
is handled for you.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:48.400
CARTER MORGAN: Mm-hmm.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:49.810
Can I ask a question?

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:51.977
Brian, I see that you've
got something on your mind,

00:06:51.977 --> 00:06:54.490
so let me sneak this one in.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:56.950
I'm very used to with, say,
Kubernetes, just putting

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.620
a web app in a container
and running that.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.998
What is the difference
between Cloud Run, which

00:07:01.998 --> 00:07:04.540
I'm not really familiar with,
and something like a Kubernetes

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.780
there?

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.270
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Cloud Run
is just managed Kubernetes.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.640
Shh.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:16.070
CARTER MORGAN: Oh, so
then GKE, basically.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.140
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
Yeah, Cloud Run

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.570
runs on top of
Knative, which itself

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:24.620
runs on top of GKE, which
itself runs on top of VMs, which

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:27.110
itself runs in Google
data centers, which itself

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:28.880
runs on servers.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:32.060
You don't have to worry
about so many things

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:34.610
when you move up this
abstraction stack.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:38.140
You're still using the
underlying technology,

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.623
but you don't have
to worry about it.

00:07:40.623 --> 00:07:42.040
BRIAN DORSEY: I
love what you just

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.945
said there-- abstraction stack.

00:07:43.945 --> 00:07:45.550
I like to think about it--

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.700
you talk about Cloud Run, and
we've got App Engine and--

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:49.330
or, sorry.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:50.705
You were talking
about functions,

00:07:50.705 --> 00:07:53.080
and we've got App Engine
and Cloud Run and Kubernetes

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:53.980
and VMs.

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:56.405
And like you said, if you've
got a greenfield thing,

00:07:56.405 --> 00:07:58.780
you probably want to start at
the top of this abstraction

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:00.650
stack and work your way down.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.010
And if you have something
existing that's already

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.990
running in a VM or on
a computer somewhere,

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:06.740
it's probably going
to be easiest to start

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.750
towards the bottom.

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:09.250
Nice.

00:08:12.045 --> 00:08:13.670
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
Yeah, it's very nice.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.250
I remember, back
in the day, if I

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:20.690
wanted to try to get
logs or debug a machine,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.630
I would have to physically go
to the data center and wheel

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:28.070
out a little trolley and
plug in a keyboard and mouse.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:31.580
Nowadays, depending on
where in the stack I am,

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:33.830
I either SSH directly
into the machine,

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:37.409
or I go into Cloud Logging, and
everything is already there.

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:41.400
I don't have to physically drive
to where my machine is anymore.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.039
I don't have to
worry about that.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:45.410
My life is very much
better now I do not

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.770
have to physically go and
plug in keyboard and mouse

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:51.140
into servers.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.700
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:54.410
Yeah, I can relate to this.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:57.500
Being in the military, I used to
work on some very old computer

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.270
technology.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.610
I'm glad I don't have to
deal with that anymore.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.902
But there's something I wanted
to dig into a little bit

00:09:03.902 --> 00:09:04.760
deeper here.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.223
Your thought process
behind thinking about,

00:09:07.223 --> 00:09:09.140
well, what do you want
to have to worry about,

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.060
and what do you have to manage--

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:14.960
is that based on what resources
the application is touching?

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.550
For example, if
the application is

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.590
touching a web port
or a WebSocket,

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:23.282
then you might have to use Cloud
Run as opposed to a serverless.

00:09:23.282 --> 00:09:24.490
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: It depends.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.540
If you have a system that
relies on specific GPUs,

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.250
you might have to
choose a service

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.130
where you can define those.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:42.120
The Cloud Run container
contract is just, tell me

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:45.090
what port on this container
I should listen on,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.710
and I will serve that for you.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:51.060
You can have anything you
want inside that container.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.060
I have colleagues
that have put Fortran

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.790
into that container and
COBOL into that container.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.740
As long as it listens
on a port, it's fine.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.690
But if you need
to start worrying

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.000
about not just one container,
but multiple containers,

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.830
you might want to
drop down into GKE

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.170
and handle your
orchestration at that level.

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:14.830
If you want to have to worry
about specific types of CPU

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:17.140
architecture, you might
need to drop down into VMs.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:20.770
But if all you
want to do is, when

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:27.190
someone goes to my HTTP URL,
they get back a response,

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.110
then some of these
higher-level abstractions

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.430
are going to be the
solution for you.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:33.080
But you don't just
have to choose one.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:36.000
You can mix and match.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.560
CARTER MORGAN: Ooh, Brian.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.570
BRIAN DORSEY: OK,
so actually I'm

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:43.440
inspired by Katie's
picture-painting here,

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.520
and the it depends.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.670
So what if we--

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.590
we'll keep with
the theme of VMs,

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.320
because that's
this conversation.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.410
But compare and contrast.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.548
If you were going to
use functions versus VM,

00:10:58.548 --> 00:11:00.090
when would you use
functions instead?

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:01.215
When would you choose a VM?

00:11:01.215 --> 00:11:03.270
When would you combine them?

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:07.290
And go through the whole
abstraction stack one at a time

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:09.948
and dig into each.

00:11:09.948 --> 00:11:11.490
CARTER MORGAN: I am
so here for this,

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.080
because as soon
as you said that,

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:14.715
my first question was, oh, wait.

00:11:14.715 --> 00:11:16.830
I can use serverless
and VMs together

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:17.910
in the same application?

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:18.675
What?

00:11:18.675 --> 00:11:19.810
Is that a thing?

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:21.780
So yes, I want to go,
and I want to find out

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:27.060
what tools I can use in place,
or begrudgingly with, VMs,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:28.753
if that's what I need to do.

00:11:28.753 --> 00:11:29.670
BRIAN DORSEY: Awesome.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:31.253
Katie, would you be
up for coming back

00:11:31.253 --> 00:11:33.157
and dig deeper on some of these?

00:11:33.157 --> 00:11:34.740
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
(CHANTING) Season 3.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.790
Season 3.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:38.235
I'd love to.

00:11:38.235 --> 00:11:39.610
BRIAN DORSEY: Take
that as a yes.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:41.190
